   "id": "k2-56",
   "name": "Action of electricity on solid compounds"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Ionic compounds such as common salt (sodium chloride), lead(II) bromide and copper(II) chloride are made of charged particles called ions.\n• In the solid state the ions are held in fixed positions, so the solid does not conduct.\n• When the compound is melted (molten) or dissolved in water, the ions are free to move, so it conducts and is decomposed by the current. This decomposition by electricity is called electrolysis.\nCovalent compounds such as sugar, wax and ethanol do not conduct, even when melted.\nExample: molten sodium chloride → sodium (at the negative electrode) + chlorine (at the positive electrode): 2NaCl → 2Na + Cl₂.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Why does solid salt not conduct electricity, while molten salt does?",
  "steps": [
   "Salt is made of ions.",
   "In the solid the ions cannot move; in the melt they are free to move and carry the current."
  ],
  "answer": "In the solid the ions are fixed; when molten they can move.",
  "checks": []
 },
